<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.2: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and y-Intercept</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00262</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.2: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and y-Intercept</md:title>
    <md:uuid>40f6665e-5398-489e-bad6-dfcbc8f33f68</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Recall that the slope-intercept form of the equation of a line is \(y=mx+b\), where \(m\) is the slope and \(b\) is the \(𝑦\)-intercept. The \(𝑦\)-intercept is the point where the graph crosses the \(𝑦\)-axis. For \(𝑦\)-intercept \(𝑏\), the coordinates of the point are \((0,𝑏)\).</para>

<para>In the following activity, you will complete a series of questions regarding this information.</para>

<note class="link-to-learning">
    <para>Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
  </note></section>

  <note class="self-check">
    <para>Write the equation of a line in slope-intercept form with a slope of \( - \frac{3}{4} \) and a \( y \)-intercept of \( -2 \).</para>
  </note>

  <section><title>Additional Resources</title>
  <section><title>Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
    <para>We can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \( y=mx+b \). Now we will do the reverse—we will start with the slope and \( y \)-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>

<section><title>Example 1 </title>

    <para>Find the equation of a line with slope \( −1 \) and \( y \)-intercept \( (0,−3) \).</para>
    <para>
      <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
   </para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Name the slope. 
\( m = -1 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Name the \( y \)-intercept. 
\( b = -3 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \).
\( y = -1x + -3 \) 
\( y = -x-3 \)</para>
    <para>The same information can be picked up from a graph of a line.</para></section>
    <section>
      <title>Example 2 </title>

    <para>Find the equation of the line shown in the graph.</para>
    <para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_14_05_01.png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
   </para>
    <para>
      <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
   </para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope by counting the rise and run.
\( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) 
From \( (0, -4) \) to \( (3,-2) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{2}{3} \).</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. 
\( y \)-intercept \( (0, -4) \)
\( b = -4 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \). 
\( y = mx +b \)
\( y = \frac{2}{3} x - 4 \)</para></section>

<note class="try">
  <title>Try It: Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the equation of the line graphed below in slope-intercept form:</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="This figure has a graph of a straight line on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 8 to 8. ">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_14_05_02.png"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
          <para>Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:
  Here is how to write the equation of the line in slope-intercept form.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Find the slope by counting the rise and run.
  \( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) 
  From \( (0, 1) \) to \( (5, 4) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{3}{5} \).</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. 
  \( y \)-intercept \( (0, 1) \)
  \( b = 1 \)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b\).
  \( y = mx +b \)
  \( y = \frac{3}{5} x + 1 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise></note>
  </section></section>
</content>
